-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Memory Constraints:** Naive differentiable rendering of high-resolution images is intractable due to memory limits. The patch-based rendering with stride is required to mitigate this [S1].
+**Pitfalls**
+*   **Memory Constraints:** Naive differentiable rendering of high-resolution images is intractable due to memory limits [S1]. The patch-based approach with reduced sampling is required [S1].
 *   **Mode Collapse:** The absolute directional CLIP loss can cause mode collapse of the generator and hurt generation diversity [S2].
-*   **Insufficient Magnitude:** Directional loss only measures directional similarity between normalized vectors, ignoring magnitude. This can result in weak or insufficient style transfer [S2]. The relative loss or global contrastive loss may be needed to ensure proper stylization magnitude [S2].
-*   **Non-uniform Stylization:** CLIP may focus more on regions with distinguishable features, leading to excess stylization in some parts and insufficiency in others (e.g., eyes vs. faces) [S2].
+*   **Insufficient Magnitude:** Directional losses alone may struggle to reach sufficient stylization magnitude, resulting in weak style transfer [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Limited by the number of iterations (500) and the cost of rendering patches. The use of reduced sampling points (16) and small patches ($128 \times 128$) reduces per-iteration cost compared to full-image rendering [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced by rendering patches instead of full images. The stride of 5 and limited sampling points further constrain memory usage [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables text-driven stylization of 3D scenes. The patch-based approach makes it feasible to run on standard hardware [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** May suffer from mode collapse (absolute loss) or insufficient stylization magnitude (directional loss). Non-uniform stylization across the scene is a known issue [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When text-guided artistic stylization of a NeRF is required, and computational resources are limited [S1, S2].
-*   **When not to use:** When photorealistic reconstruction is the primary goal, or when uniform stylization across the entire scene is critical without additional post-processing [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Proportional to the number of iterations (500) and the cost of rendering patches with 16 samples per ray [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced compared to full-image rendering by using $128 \times 128$ patches and fewer sampling points [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables text-guided stylization of NeRFs; memory-efficient via patch rendering [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Absolute loss risks mode collapse; directional loss may lack sufficient magnitude for strong stylization [S2].
 
 ## Background
-CLIPNeRF was introduced by Wang et al. in 2021 as an extension of StyleCLIP and StyleGAN-NADA to the domain of Neural Radiance Fields [S2]. It leverages the pre-trained CLIP model to guide the stylization process by aligning the rendered image embeddings with text prompt embeddings in the shared latent space [S1, S2]. The method addresses the challenge of editing 3D scenes with natural language descriptions, building on the principles of differentiable volume rendering [S1].
+The CLIPNeRF training configuration originates from the work on text-driven NeRF stylization, extending concepts from StyleCLIP and StyleGAN-NADA to 3D scene representations [S2]. It was introduced to address the challenge of editing NeRFs using natural language prompts while maintaining 3D consistency [S1, S2]. The method was further refined in NeRF-Art to address issues with magnitude and diversity in stylization [S2].
 
